  "name": "06 — Presence: Social",
  "text": "Two accounts, two audiences, deliberately separate. One sells the service; one recruits the people who deliver it.\n\n@relevestaffing\n\nAudience: founders and business owners who need help and have not hired offshore before.\n\nJob: build trust. Answer the two real hesitations: can I trust this person to handle it, and will they add work instead of removing it. Content pillars: how we vet (the six fronts), what “handled” looks like week to week, the right-resume-wrong-fit story, and the Client and Talent Success Managers at work. Never cost-led hooks.\n\nBio: “Consider it handled. / Virtual executive staff, vetted on six fronts and fully managed. / Book a discovery call ↓” → relevestaffing.com\n\n@relevecareers\n\nAudience: offshore talent looking for real careers with real executives.\n\nJob: attract the calibre the promise depends on. Tagline “Take the watch.” — but always paired with a plain explanation of what Relève Careers actually is.\n\nOwn logo lockup in the same system: RELÈVE with CAREERS as the ruled descriptor, plus a square avatar.\n\nChannels in use\n\nInstagram — @relevestaffingPrimary. Linked from the site footer.\n\nLive\n\nInstagram — @relevecareersTalent-facing. Exists; the footer points at the main account.\n\nLive\n\nFacebookPage 61593520618684.\n\nLive\n\nLinkedIn/company/relèveexecutivestaffing. Founder-role copy written for Sage.\n\nLive\n\nPosting planRequested for both accounts. Content style reference for careers: The Grapevine Agency (@grapevineagency) — bespoke corporate and domestic staffing, LA and NY.\n\nTo build\n\nEvery piece must say the staff are virtual. “Executive Staffing” reads as local placement unless the post says otherwise.\n\nThe three looks (8 November 2026, Nona)\n\nNona chose all three directions from the framework canvas (Design artifact “Relève Social Framework”). Every Instagram and Facebook post takes one look, by day: A B C A B C, shifted each week, so no two days in a row and none of the top three grid tiles match.\n\nA · The Wall: The website’s plaster wall with window light on covers, last slides and single posts (three photographs: the hero wall, the wall with a sprig, the evening wall), white logo, Lora headline low in the dark of the wall. Inside pages on limewash paper with the fern logo, a fine rule and page numbers; step pages carry a large pale step numeral.\n\nB · The Edition: A magazine. Cover: cream page, a photographic plate with an italic caption (Plate I), the subtitle in a side column, an oversized Lora headline below. Inside pages alternate a fern spread and a cream page with folios; a step sets as two columns. Last slide and single statements: cream headline block over a full-width plate.\n\nC · The Object: The page as a real sheet of paper on linen, stone, deep plaster or palm-shadowed plaster, slightly turned, with real shadow; inside slides show a second sheet beneath. A phone-note, search or comment single post never sits on paper (card in a card): it takes The Edition that day.\n\nEverywhere: Lora headlines over Poppins text (Geist retired from graphics). The real logo and the “Consider it handled.” script image. The same layouts (statement, rules, number, compare, notes, search, flags, quiz, comment) in every look. The LinkedIn Brief’s cover follows the day’s look; its inside pages stay the report. Hiring posts keep their own framework.\n\nCode: dirFor(), wallEl(), editionEl(), objectEl() and the Brief’s briefMasthead() in lib/content-render.tsx; photographs in public/social/frames/; fonts in public/fonts/. Commit 579164e. Local renders: set RENDER_ASSET_DIR to the app’s public folder.\n\nCreative direction standard (permanent, 7 November 2026, Nona)\n\nNona’s standing brief for every Relève visual, issued as section 11, “Creative Direction & Social Media Design Intelligence”. Claude acts as Relève’s Creative Director. It sits on top of the Relève Design Standard (lib/design-standard.ts, project doc claude/design-standard-2026-11-03.md): the Design Standard sets the identity (fern, plaster, silver, Lora and Poppins, the real logo), and this standard sets how each campaign is researched, varied and reviewed.\n\nCovers: Instagram carousels and statics, LinkedIn carousels, documents and graphics, campaigns, educational graphics, infographics, lead magnets, downloadable guides, presentations, promotions, website graphics and every other marketing asset.\n\nResearch first: Before any new campaign or significant series: research current trends (this year, weighted to the last 30 to 90 days) in Instagram carousel and LinkedIn document design, editorial layout, type, colour, art direction, motion and new formats, studying real work from premium consulting, executive services, hospitality, wellness and luxury brands. Sort findings into three: relevant to Relève, popular but off-position, timeless and kept. Present research with links, keep observed trends separate from performance claims, and never say a design will perform because it looks good.\n\nThe bar: Art-directed by an experienced editorial team: premium international publications, luxury branding, high-end business titles, architectural and lifestyle photography, independent studios, private-client hospitality. Original, never copied. Strong composition, exceptional type, intentional negative space, nuanced colour, refined texture, considered asymmetry.\n\nInstagram: Carousels open on an editorial cover with a short, strong headline; every slide has one job; the set builds; layouts alternate; text slides are balanced with photography, diagrams and worked examples; nothing crowded; every slide reads on a phone; the last slide is designed, with one next step. Treatments rotate: type-led covers, photographic storytelling, annotated visuals, data-led and educational layouts. Check current platform sizes before finalising.\n\nLinkedIn: Its own creative direction, never a reused Instagram graphic. Reads as intellectual authority: carousels as short editorial reports, typeset document layouts, opinion-led covers, minimal data storytelling, diagrams, frameworks, founder photography. Mobile first, polished on desktop. Document carousels ship as a proper multi-page PDF with consistent page numbers, margins and type. Never a PowerPoint look. Decided 7 November 2026 (Nona): LinkedIn gets its own version of each day’s post, The Relève Brief (see the Decisions log).\n\nVariation: No default cover, centred headline, background, three-card row, icon set, text-and-photo placement or ornament. A flexible system of approved compositions, each piece built around its message: some type-led, some photographic, editorial, educational, data-led or diagrammatic. The feed reads as curated. (Hiring posts keep their one fixed framework, by Nona’s 6 November call.)\n\nReview before delivery: Ten questions, on top of the Design Standard’s check: at home in a premium editorial publication; custom, not template; exceptional type; interesting, balanced composition; message lands at once; readable on a phone; consistent with Relève; current without leaning on a trend; nothing cheap, generic or AI-made; presentable as a premium agency’s work. Any weakness is fixed before she sees it.\n\nOngoing: Reassess the direction periodically against current design, platform changes and exceptional work; flag when the brand needs to evolve without losing its core. The aim: one of the most recognisable, visually sophisticated brands in its category. Monthly design review (Nona, 7 November 2026): a scheduled task on the 1st of each month at 1:31 PM Pacific researches current design with links, writes claude/design-review-YYYY-MM.md to the project and sends Nona a short summary. It changes nothing on its own; recommendations wait for her yes."
